i915: let eDP panels advertise modes smaller than their fixed mode

i915 prunes every mode on an eDP connector whose resolution differs from the
panel's fixed mode, so a 4K laptop panel offers 3840x2160 and nothing else.
On a UHD 620 driving 3840x2160 that is a problem: there is no way to ask the
GPU to composite fewer pixels.

The hardware and the rest of the driver already do what we want. When a
smaller mode is accepted, intel_panel_compute_config() overwrites
adjusted_mode with the panel's fixed mode — so the eDP link still runs at the
panel's native timing — while crtc_state->pipe_src keeps the requested size,
and pch_panel_fitting() programs the panel fitter to scale pipe_src up to
adjusted_mode. Only the mode list check stands in the way.

So relax that check from "differs" to "larger". Modes bigger than the panel
are still rejected, and the existing refresh-rate check is untouched, which
keeps intel_panel_compute_config()'s own +/-1 Hz guard meaningful.

Pair this with video=eDP-1:<smaller mode> to actually get an extra entry in
the list; the EDID of a fixed panel normally supplies only its native timing.

Not upstream — local to this machine.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_panel.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_panel.c
@@ -404,10 +404,10 @@
 	if (!fixed_mode)
 		return MODE_OK;
 
-	if (mode->hdisplay != fixed_mode->hdisplay)
+	if (mode->hdisplay > fixed_mode->hdisplay)
 		return MODE_PANEL;
 
-	if (mode->vdisplay != fixed_mode->vdisplay)
+	if (mode->vdisplay > fixed_mode->vdisplay)
 		return MODE_PANEL;
 
 	if (drm_mode_vrefresh(mode) != drm_mode_vrefresh(fixed_mode))
